<meta type=article>
  <title | Theorem Family Sweep>
</meta>

<section | Statements>
The propositional family shares one counter; definition and example each own a
counter; remark and proof are unnumbered by default.

<!-- cell: theorem/form:pipe -->
<theorem | For every prime $p$ there are infinitely many primes congruent to $1 \pmod p$.>

<!-- cell: theorem/kwarg:name -->
<theorem name="Pythagoras" | In a right triangle, $a^2 + b^2 = c^2$.>

<!-- cell: lemma/form:pipe -->
<lemma | Every bounded monotone sequence of reals converges.>

<!-- cell: corollary/form:pipe -->
<corollary | A continuous function on a closed interval attains its maximum.>

<!-- cell: proposition/form:pipe -->
<proposition | The empty set is a subset of every set.>

<!-- cell: definition/form:pipe -->
<definition | A set is countable if it is finite or in bijection with the naturals.>

<!-- cell: example/form:pipe -->
<example | The set of rational numbers is countable.>

<!-- cell: remark/form:pipe -->
<remark | The same argument generalises to any countable union of countable sets.>

<!-- cell: proof/form:long -->
<proof>
The claim follows directly from the definitions given above.
</proof>
